🧩 Why templates must be defined in headers
1️⃣ Template functions/classes are not compiled immediately

When you write:

template<typename T>
void print(T val) {
    std::cout << val;
}


the compiler does not generate code yet.
It only creates a blueprint — the actual function is generated only when instantiated (i.e., used with a concrete type).

print(10);     // instantiates print<int>
print("hi");   // instantiates print<const char*>


So each translation unit (TU) that uses print() needs access to the full definition to generate its own instantiation.

2️⃣ If you define a template in a .cpp file
// print.cpp
template<typename T>
void print(T val) {
    std::cout << val;
}


and only declare it in a header:

// print.h
template<typename T>
void print(T val);


then when another file does:

#include "print.h"
print(10);


👉 The compiler can’t find the definition — because it’s hidden in another TU (print.cpp), and templates are instantiated at compile time, not link time.
Hence, you get:

undefined reference to `void print<int>(int)`

3️⃣ The correct way

Put the full template definition in the header:

// print.h
#pragma once
#include <iostream>

template<typename T>
void print(T val) {
    std::cout << val;
}